---
/**
 * Compact lesson excerpt for reviewing the DIBS Slab, DIBS Sans, and Fira Code roles together.
 * The Spanish explanation adapts the Kotlin lesson's “Trailing lambdas (λ al final)” section.
 * The paired calls preserve its distinction between conventional and trailing-lambda syntax.
 *
 * The parent fixture supplies local DIBS faces and the same Google Fonts stylesheet as production.
 * The browser spec waits for the stylesheet and faces, checks Chromium's rendered fonts, and
 * captures this region at desktop and mobile viewports.
 */

const codeSample = [
  "// Conventional function arguments",
  "list",
  "    .filter({ x -> x > 10 })",
  "    .map({ x -> x * 2 })",
  "",
  "// Trailing lambdas",
  "list",
  "    .filter { x -> x > 10 }",
  "    .map { x -> x * 2 }",
].join("\n");
---

<section
  class="three-role-system-review" data-fixture-section="combined"
  data-visual-review="three-role-system" aria-labelledby="three-role-system-heading"
>
  <h2
    id="three-role-system-heading" data-font-family="dibs-slab"
    data-typography-role="heading" style="font-weight: 700"
  >
    Trailing lambdas (λ al final)
  </h2>

  <p
    data-font-family="dibs-sans" data-typography-role="prose"
    style="font-weight: 400"
  >
    En Kotlin, si el <strong data-font-family="dibs-sans" style="font-weight: 700"
    >último parámetro</strong> de una función tiene un <em
      data-font-family="dibs-sans" style="font-weight: 400; font-style: italic"
    >function type</em>, puedes pasar la lambda fuera de los paréntesis. Esta forma se conoce como{
      " "
    }
    <span
      data-font-family="dibs-sans" data-typography-state="500-normal"
      style="font-weight: 500"
    >trailing lambda</span>
    {" "}y facilita la lectura de operaciones sobre colecciones.
  </p>

  <p
    data-font-family="dibs-sans" data-typography-role="prose"
    style="font-weight: 400"
  >
    <strong data-font-family="dibs-sans" style="font-weight: 700"
    >Si la lambda es el único parámetro, también puedes omitir los paréntesis.</strong>
    La expresión
    <code
      data-font-family="fira-code" data-typography-role="inline-code"
      style="font-weight: 400; font-style: normal"
    >x -&gt; x * 2</code> muestra una lambda que duplica su entrada.
  </p>

  <h3
    data-font-family="dibs-slab" data-typography-role="subheading"
    style="font-weight: 500"
  >
    Sintaxis básica
  </h3>

  <p
    data-font-family="dibs-sans" data-typography-role="prose"
    style="font-weight: 400"
  >
    Las dos llamadas aplican la misma transformación; en la segunda, la lambda queda al final de
    cada llamada:
  </p>

  <pre
    data-font-family="fira-code" data-typography-role="code-block"
    style="font-weight: 400; font-style: normal"
  ><code data-font-family="fira-code">{codeSample}</code></pre>
</section>

<style>
  .three-role-system-review {
    box-sizing: border-box;
    width: min(100%, 72ch);
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: 1.25rem;
    border: 1px solid currentColor;
    font-synthesis: none;
  }

  .three-role-system-review h2 {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    font-size: var(--text-2xl);
    line-height: 1.2;
  }

  .three-role-system-review h3 {
    margin: 1.25rem 0 0.5rem;
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    line-height: 1.35;
  }

  .three-role-system-review p {
    margin: 0.75rem 0;
    font-size: var(--content-font-size);
    line-height: var(--content-line-height);
  }

  .three-role-system-review p code {
    border-radius: 0.25rem;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, currentColor 10%, transparent);
    padding: 0.08em 0.28em;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .three-role-system-review pre {
    box-sizing: border-box;
    width: 100%;
    margin: 0.75rem 0 0;
    padding: 1rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    white-space: pre;
    tab-size: 4;
  }
</style>
